Unit Testing
-> Scope: tests individual classes/methods in isolation
-> Dependencies: mocked
-> Spring context: not loaded

Integration Testing
-> Scope: tests how multiple layers (controller->service->repository->db) work together
-> Dependencies: real beans, real Db(H2/TestContainers), real serialization
-> Spring context: Fully or partially loaded

TestContainers
-> java library that provides lightweight, disposable docker containers for integration testing
-> once tests finish, these containers are automatically destroyed, ensuring a clean environment every time

Annotations (integration testing)

@SpringBootTest
-> tells spring boot to start full application context for testing

@MockMvc
-> fake postman
-> allows to test controller endpoints without starting a real http server(tomcat)
-> requests sent via mockMvc.perform(...) are processed through DispatcherServlet

@AutoConfigureMockMvc
-> configures mockmvc bean

Dependencies to use for testcontainers
1. testcontainers
2. postgresql testcontainers

Annotations (testcontainers)

@Testcontainers
@Container

@DynamicPropertySource
-> injects container values into Spring’s environment at runtime

@ServiceConnection
-> SpringBoot 3.1+
-> spring boot automatically detects container values and injects them

Why db properties are not provided in application-test.properties?
-> testcontainers generates random port each time it starts a container
   (jdbc url changes dynamically)
